Ole Andersen schrieb:
> 
> The scroll of magic mapping never has any negative effect*.

For many items, cursedness just lets the object perform the same
effect at a less desirable degree[1] - a very few examples would be
scrolls of identify, potions of *healing and tinning kits.

[1] this sounds like bad english; i hope you get the idea, though.

I think the idea of having more "reversed" effects for cursed items
isn't entirely new, and i don't find it neat, either, sorry.

> A cursed ?SoMM could blind you and wake up all monsters on the level.

Seems completely unrelated to me. Blindness could be argued, but
non-cursed scrolls of magic mapping don't grant knowledge about the
presence of monsters.

> Reading ?SoMM while confused could give you the map of a previously
> explored level - except for what you can see with your own eyes. When
> you explore the level personally afterwards, the map will have to
> change, of course.

Some more fiddling with the confused effects could be interesting.
Mapping a randomly-picked previously-visited level sounds nice - not
overwriting your current level, but rather have the level itself
mapped; this would only be helpful if the level wasn't explored
entirely or if its map was sucked away by a mind flayer.

Giving you a fake map still sounds nice, but i think it'd be more
fitting for a confused cursed scroll. Anyone know how difficult this
would be to code?

